concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "5E Serious contraventions"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-5e ;
    values:originalText "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a contravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that:  (a) is a serious offence; or  (b) is an offence punishable:  (i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of at least 3 years; or  (ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or  (iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual— by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty units; or  (c) could, if established, render the person committing the contravention liable:  (i) if the contravention were committed by an individual— to pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,  Stored communications  Schedule 1 Principal amendments  Part 1  Telecommunications (Interception) Amendment Act 2006                   5 or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 180 penalty units or more; or  (ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an individual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty units or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 900 penalty units or more.  (2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or a contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of this Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that kind, as the case may be, that:  (a) has been committed or is being committed; or  (b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been committed, of being committed or of being likely to be committed.  (3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty units in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory includes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty that is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to that number of penalty units. 5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications system  (1) For the purposes of this Act, a communication:  (a) is taken to start passing over a telecommunications system when it is sent or transmitted by the person sending the communication; and  (b) is taken to continue to pass over the system until it becomes accessible to the intended recipient of the communication.  (2) However, if a communication is sent from an address on a computer network operated by or on behalf of the Australian Federal Police, it is taken not to start passing over a telecommunications system, for the purposes of this Act, until it is no longer under the control of any of the following:  (a) any AFP employee responsible for operating, protecting and maintaining the network;  (b) any AFP employee responsible for enforcement of the professional standards of the Australian Federal Police.  Schedule 1  Stored communications" ;
    skos:definition "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a contravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that:  (a) is a serious offence; or  (b) is an offence punishable:  (i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of at least 3 years; or  (ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or  (iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual— by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty units; or  (c) could, if established, render the person committing the contravention liable:  (i) if the contravention were committed by an individual— to pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,  Stored communications  Schedule 1 Principal amendments  Part 1  Telecommunications (Interception) Amendment Act 2006                   5 or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 180 penalty units or more; or  (ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an individual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty units or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 900 penalty units or more.  (2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or a contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of this Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that kind, as the case may be, that:  (a) has been committed or is being committed; or  (b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been committed, of being committed or of being likely to be committed.  (3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty units in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory includes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty that is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to that number of penalty units. 5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications system  (1) For the purposes of this Act, a communication:  (a) is taken to start"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "22105d38a67ad79d9a7e12033d98e900dd31a6d5f9f62c4abd795a405dd24bd4" ;
    cof:pageNumber "8"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006-sch-1-sec-5e-ss-2 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006.pdf> ;
    dc:source "telecommunications-interception-amendment-act-2006.pdf, p.8" .
doc:sch-1-sec-5e a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "5E Serious contraventions" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a contravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that:  (a) is a serious offence; or  (b) is an offence punishable:  (i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of at least 3 years; or  (ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or  (iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual— by a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty units; or  (c) could, if established, render the person committing the contravention liable:  (i) if the contravention were committed by an individual— to pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,  Stored communications  Schedule 1 Principal amendments  Part 1  Telecommunications (Interception) Amendment Act 2006                   5 or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 180 penalty units or more; or  (ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an individual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty units or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of 900 penalty units or more.  (2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or a contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of this Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that kind, as the case may be, that:  (a) has been committed or is being committed; or  (b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been committed, of being committed or of being likely to be committed.  (3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty units in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory includes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty that is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to that number of penalty units. 5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications system  (1) For the purposes of this Act, a communication:  (a) is taken to start passing over a telecommunications system when it is sent or transmitted by the person sending the communication; and  (b) is taken to continue to pass over the system until it becomes accessible to the intended recipient of the communication.  (2) However, if a communication is sent from an address on a computer network operated by or on behalf of the Australian Federal Police, it is taken not to start passing over a telecommunications system, for the purposes of this Act, until it is no longer under the control of any of the following:  (a) any AFP employee responsible for operating, protecting and maintaining the network;  (b) any AFP employee responsible for enforcement of the professional standards of the Australian Federal Police.  Schedule 1  Stored communications" ;
    cof:pageNumber "8"^^xsd:integer .

